<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pierre Curie</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1859">1859</a>–<a href="/years/1906">1906</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pierre Curie was a French physicist and chemist, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, who was a pioneer in crystallography, magnetism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radioactivity">radioactivity</a>. In 1880 he and his older brother Jacques demonstrated piezoelectricity, and his doctoral work on magnetism gave his name to Curie's law and the Curie temperature. With his wife, Maria Skłodowska-Curie, he worked to isolate polonium and radium, and the couple were the first to use the term radioactivity; they shared one half of the 1903 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Physics, the first married couple to win a Nobel Prize. In 1906 he slipped while crossing the Rue Dauphine in Paris and was killed under a horse-drawn cart.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pierre-curie.jpg" alt="Pierre Curie" width="280" height="396" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nobel Foundation · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PierreCurie.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Working in a Paris shed, Marie and <b>Pierre Curie</b> detected a new, intensely radioactive element in pitchblende, announced days later as radium.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-21">21 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>A paper presented to the French Academy of Sciences announced radium, a new element found in pitchblende by Marie and <b>Pierre Curie</b> with Gustave Bémont.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-26">26 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>He had discovered radioactivity, soon investigated by Marie and <b>Pierre Curie</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-1">1 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
